From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 04:10:52 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] venus: keep the ring awake while a driver notify still covers
 it

The driver announces commands to an idle ring with a notify, and sends
at most one per idle timeout: Mesa throttles by the same
VN_RING_IDLE_TIMEOUT_NS it passes as idleTimeout, counts that notify as
covering every command it submits in the interval, and never notifies
again while it waits for the ring. The ring cleared pending_notify at
every idle transition, so a notify that arrived while it was busy was
dropped. When the ring had already read the notified command on its own,
a later command inside the driver's interval that found the ring idle was
never announced; the ring slept on it and the driver waited until it
aborted. A Vulkan CTS image-format family hung that way in 3 of 15 runs
on a Darwin host.

The ring now never sleeps while a notify the driver counts as
outstanding may still cover a later command. It takes a notify under the
ring mutex, both when it wakes and at an idle transition, instead of
discarding it; a taken notify keeps it awake for twice the idle timeout,
the driver's interval plus the same again as margin for the two clocks.
A notify that arrives while the ring works stays pending for the next
transition.

The tail load after setting the idle bit is now sequentially consistent.
The driver stores the tail and then loads the status; the ring sets the
idle bit and then loads the tail. Each must see the other's store, and
an acquire load (LDAPR after LDSETAL on arm64) may be satisfied before
the idle bit is visible.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/venus/vkr_ring.c | 69 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 60 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/venus/vkr_ring.c b/src/venus/vkr_ring.c
index 26446485cbd480ec71f71e899695bd19c071977f..1911c07c931aa7e7ce5e690ee2edf631ed0bb3c8 100644
--- a/src/venus/vkr_ring.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_ring.c
@@ -75,6 +75,43 @@ vkr_ring_load_tail(const struct vkr_ring *ring)
    return atomic_load_explicit(ring->control.tail, memory_order_acquire);
 }
 
+static uint32_t
+vkr_ring_load_tail_after_idle(const struct vkr_ring *ring)
+{
+   /* The driver stores the tail and then loads the status, and the ring sets
+    * the idle bit and then loads the tail: each side must see the other's
+    * store, or the driver skips the notify while the ring goes to sleep on a
+    * command it never read. That needs both loads sequentially consistent. An
+    * acquire load may be satisfied before the idle bit is visible (LDAPR after
+    * LDSETAL on arm64), so this one is not an acquire load.
+    */
+   return atomic_load_explicit(ring->control.tail, memory_order_seq_cst);
+}
+
+static uint64_t
+vkr_ring_notify_window(const struct vkr_ring *ring)
+{
+   /* The driver sends at most one notify per idle timeout -- Mesa throttles
+    * by the VN_RING_IDLE_TIMEOUT_NS it also passes as idleTimeout -- and
+    * counts that notify as covering every command it submits within the
+    * interval, measured on its own clock from its decision to notify. The
+    * ring therefore stays awake for twice the idle timeout after it takes a
+    * notify: one interval for the driver's window, which began before the
+    * notify reached the ring, and one of margin for the two clocks.
+    */
+   return ring->idle_timeout * 2;
+}
+
+static bool
+vkr_ring_take_notify(struct vkr_ring *ring)
+{
+   mtx_lock(&ring->mutex);
+   const bool notified = ring->pending_notify;
+   ring->pending_notify = false;
+   mtx_unlock(&ring->mutex);
+   return notified;
+}
+
 static void
 vkr_ring_unset_status_bits(struct vkr_ring *ring, uint32_t mask)
 {
@@ -264,17 +301,27 @@ vkr_ring_thread(void *arg)
 #endif
    }
 
-   uint64_t last_submit = vkr_ring_now();
+   /* The ring never sleeps while a notify the driver counts as outstanding
+    * may still cover a later command: a taken notify keeps it awake for the
+    * notify window, and one that arrives while it is busy is taken at the
+    * next idle transition rather than discarded there.
+    */
+   uint64_t idle_deadline = vkr_ring_now() + ring->idle_timeout;
    uint32_t relax_iter = 0;
    int ret = 0;
    while (ring->started) {
       bool wait = false;
-      if (vkr_ring_now() >= last_submit + ring->idle_timeout) {
-         ring->pending_notify = false;
-         vkr_ring_set_status_bits(ring, VK_RING_STATUS_IDLE_BIT_MESA);
-         wait = ring->buffer.cur == vkr_ring_load_tail(ring);
-         if (!wait)
-            vkr_ring_unset_status_bits(ring, VK_RING_STATUS_IDLE_BIT_MESA);
+      const uint64_t now = vkr_ring_now();
+      if (now >= idle_deadline) {
+         if (vkr_ring_take_notify(ring)) {
+            idle_deadline = now + vkr_ring_notify_window(ring);
+            relax_iter = 0;
+         } else {
+            vkr_ring_set_status_bits(ring, VK_RING_STATUS_IDLE_BIT_MESA);
+            wait = ring->buffer.cur == vkr_ring_load_tail_after_idle(ring);
+            if (!wait)
+               vkr_ring_unset_status_bits(ring, VK_RING_STATUS_IDLE_BIT_MESA);
+         }
       }
 
       if (wait) {
@@ -289,13 +336,17 @@ vkr_ring_thread(void *arg)
                goto out;
             }
          }
+         /* The notify that woke the ring is taken here; one that arrives
+          * after this point is left pending for the next idle transition.
+          */
+         ring->pending_notify = false;
          vkr_ring_unset_status_bits(ring, VK_RING_STATUS_IDLE_BIT_MESA);
          mtx_unlock(&ring->mutex);
 
          if (!ring->started)
             break;
 
-         last_submit = vkr_ring_now();
+         idle_deadline = vkr_ring_now() + vkr_ring_notify_window(ring);
          relax_iter = 0;
       }
 
@@ -316,7 +367,7 @@ vkr_ring_thread(void *arg)
             break;
          }
 
-         last_submit = vkr_ring_now();
+         idle_deadline = MAX2(idle_deadline, vkr_ring_now() + ring->idle_timeout);
          relax_iter = 0;
       } else {
          /* Get the active wait_ring seqno first to ensure ordering. */
